task:
Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the average number of credits (`credits`) per exam for every student, then displays the 4 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `student; average`;
2. calculates the lowest score (`score`) for each month, considering only exams with student `vera`, and then prints chronologically only the entries whose value is at least 33, in the format `month: minimum` (first print the text `Result:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the ratio of the number of exams to the number of distinct students for every month, rounded to 2 decimal places, then prints chronologically only the entries whose value is at least 4.6, in the format `month - ratio`;
2. computes, for each class, its share of the number of exams over all exams, as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place, and then outputs the single entry with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `class: X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the exams by its `score`: `heavy` if it is greater than 65 and `light` otherwise. Print the number of exams in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class - count`;
2. for each student computes the number of distinct subjects, considering only exams whose subject name contains the fragment `si` and where `credits` is at least 2, and then prints the 5 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `student; distinct`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each student, its share of the total number of credits (`credits`) among exams where `score` is greater than 75, as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the 4 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `student - X%`;
2. determines the average number of credits (`credits`) per exam for every student, considering only exams from the month `2026-04`, then displays alphabetically only the entries whose value is at least 2.5, in the format `student: average`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the highest score (`score`) multiplied by 2 and rounded to 2 decimal places (define your own word `scaled` for the multiplication and rounding) for each result, and then outputs the results alphabetically, one per line in the format `result: value`;
2. for each month works out the lowest score (`score`), and then outputs the 4 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `month: minimum`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. works out the average score (`score`) for every student, considering only exams with result other than `passed`, then displays the 3 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `student: average` (first print the text `Result:`);
2. works out the highest score (`score`) for each student, and then displays the 3 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `student: maximum` (first print the text `Result:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. determines the average score (`score`) for every student, considering only exams with class `blue` or `green`, then prints alphabetically only the entries whose value is at least 61, in the format `student; average`;
2. works out the highest score (`score`) among exams with student `sam` and prints just the value (first print the text `Result:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the ratio of the total number of credits (`credits`) to the number of distinct classes for every subject, considering only exams with result other than `absent`, rounded to 2 decimal places, then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `subject: ratio`;
2. for each student determines the number of exams, considering only exams with class `green` or `red`, and then prints the single entry with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `student: count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. prints, alphabetically and without duplicates, the values of the column `student` for exams with result `failed` and where `credits` is at least 1;
2. works out the total number of credits (`credits`) among exams with student `quinn` or `zoe` and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the number of distinct students for each subject, and then prints the 2 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `subject: distinct`;
2. determines the number of distinct subjects over all exams and prints just the value (first print the text `Report:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the highest score (`score`) multiplied by 1.2 and rounded to 2 decimal places for each subject, considering only exams with result `failed`, and then prints the 5 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `subject: value`;
2. for each result computes the average score (`score`) multiplied by 1.2 and rounded to 2 decimal places, considering only exams with subject `art` or `biology`, and then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `result - value`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. for each subject works out the number of distinct students, considering only exams with student `yuri`, and then outputs the 4 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `subject: distinct`;
2. computes, for each class, the percentage of exams with result `absent` among all exams of that class, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then displays all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `class - X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the exams by its `credits`: `high` if it is at least 3, `medium` if it is at least 2, and `low` otherwise, using your own word `bracket` for the rule. Print the number of exams in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class - count`;
2. computes the number of distinct classes for each result, considering only exams with student `quinn`, and then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `result: distinct` (first print the text `Result:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each student, the percentage of exams whose subject name contains the fragment `em` among all exams of that student, considering only exams where `credits` is at most 2, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the 2 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `student - X%`;
2. classifies every one of the exams by its `score`: `large` if it is at least 76, `medium` if it is at least 49, and `small` otherwise. Print the number of exams in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`.
